## 5. Ordinary Strict Linearization Is Impossible

Suppose an ordinary line could cancel `sigma`. A one-dimensional projective
action is a scalar cochain `lambda_g`; its multiplier is the coboundary

\[
 (\delta\lambda)(g,h)=\lambda_g\lambda_h\lambda_{g+h}^{-1}.
\]

On an abelian group this multiplier has trivial commutator. Equation (3) has
nontrivial, nondegenerate commutator. Therefore no ordinary line, including
`L_shared` or any of its characters, can turn `W9` into an honest `E[3]`
vector representation.

An inverse-sigma projective module can cancel the multiplier, but the
nondegenerate finite Heisenberg representation has minimal dimension three.
That operation would add a new rank-three carrier and change the finite
readout; it is not a line lift and is not selected by the current source.

Thus the stronger interpretation of the old exit clause is closed as an exact
no-go. It must not remain listed as a theorem still awaiting construction.

